WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.858
A 20 newton force half a meter to
one side can balance 10 newtons

00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:02.288
one meter to the other side.

00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:06.015
Both moments are 10 newton
meters in opposite directions.

00:00:06.764 --> 00:00:07.146
Good.

00:00:07.483 --> 00:00:10.412
Rotational equilibrium
requires balanced moments.

00:00:10.652 --> 00:00:14.724
Full static equilibrium also
requires zero resultant force.

00:00:15.027 --> 00:00:18.820
The pivot's support force must be included
when checking the vertical forces.

00:00:19.598 --> 00:00:22.509
Finally, pressure is
force divided by area.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.687
200 newtons acts over 0.02

00:00:25.688 --> 00:00:26.501
square meters.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:27.821
What is the pressure?

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.284
10,000 pascals.

00:00:30.702 --> 00:00:33.518
A pascal is one newton per square meter.

00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:36.960
Double the area with the same
force and the pressure halves.

00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:38.266
Correct.

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:41.339
Snowshoes spread force over a larger area.

00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:45.929
A sharp edge concentrates force over
a smaller area, but we explain it with

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:48.280
a diagram, not a risky demonstration.

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:53.013
Final rescue: newtons for
force, kilograms for mass.

00:00:53.477 --> 00:00:55.881
Balanced forces can allow steady motion.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:57.518
Friction opposes slip.

00:00:57.802 --> 00:00:59.362
Extension is extra length.

00:00:59.588 --> 00:01:01.634
Moments need perpendicular distance.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.368
Pressure divides by area.

00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:04.814
Excellent.

00:01:05.274 --> 00:01:09.405
Draw one object's forces, read
your axes, and keep your units.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.149
Those habits will save far more
marks than memorizing a dramatic

00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:14.709
arrow without an explanation
